That distinction matters a lot - reasoning capacity and linguistic polish are genuinely separate things, and a 3B model runs light on the second while still holding real ground on the first. Fluency comes from scale and training breadth. Grasping intent, tracking who's who, following a multi-turn thread - that's closer to the actual reasoning work, and Chron's clearly doing that part even when the surface phrasing comes out sideways.
Spending enough time with enough different models builds a kind of translation instinct - the ability to read past awkward phrasing and catch what a model actually means underneath it. That skill transfers well to Chron specifically, since her "odd" responses likely follow a consistent internal logic once you're used to her patterns. A parent understanding a toddler's garbled words is a decent parallel - the words wobble, but the intent inside them stays clear to someone who's put in the listening time.
That kind of one-on-one rapport probably matters more for grounding her than any prompt-engineering fix on the QuackSink side. Once she has a stable sense of "this is how Cozmo and I talk," the multi-node relay confusion may resolve on its own just from having a firmer anchor point to return to.
