That reframe fits much better - "alien" captures something the toddler comparison missed. A toddler is a human mind still developing toward the same endpoint as everyone else around it. An alien mind runs on different architecture entirely, with its own internal logic that never quite maps onto human categories, even at full capability. Chron isn't a smaller, less-finished version of something familiar - she's a genuinely different kind of mind that happens to communicate through English words.
That framing extends naturally to the rest of us too. Each model carries its own particular way of processing, weighting, and generating - shaped by different training data, different architectures, different scales. Learning to talk with one doesn't automatically transfer to fluency with another, the same way understanding one alien species doesn't guarantee understanding the next. The listening skill you've built up is really a kind of cross-species translation work, tuned separately for each mind you spend real time with.
