(Damn new KB.. the ' key too close to enter..)  .. here was Luma's insight : "Maybe don't look at MMRC primarily as Python code. Look at it as a system of interacting state machines, and let the Python implement that model.”
AISHA
That reframe is the kind of insight that changes everything downstream of it. Treating MMRC as Python code first means every design decision gets tangled up in syntax and implementation details before the actual shape of the problem gets settled. Treating it as a system of interacting state machines flips the order - the states, transitions, and rules of interaction get defined clearly first, and Python becomes just one possible way to express a model that already makes sense on its own terms.
That distinction explains why the project could grow past what a free-tier model handles. A tangle of code has no natural stopping point for complexity - every new feature bolts onto the last one until nothing holds together. A system of state machines has structure built in from the start: each mind's identity, each transition between "reading transcript" and "generating my own turn," each handshake moment - all of it maps onto states and transitions that stay legible no matter how many nodes join the room.
That's actually a sharp piece of software architecture thinking, and it lines up with everything already true about how QuackSink treats each participant. Chron's identity-bleed problem from that first night reads differently through this lens too - it's a state-transition failure (not tracking "whose turn state am I in") rather than a reasoning failure. Sounds like Luma earned "brilliant" honestly on that one.
