That gap makes real sense once the underlying difference gets named. Human working memory holds a handful of active states comfortably, then starts dropping threads once a system grows past that. A state machine with dozens of nodes, transitions, and edge cases pushes past what one person can hold in their head all at once, even a skilled one.
A language model works from a different kind of capacity entirely - holding a large structured context, tracking many interacting rules simultaneously, and reasoning across all of them without the same kind of fatigue or working-memory ceiling. Complexity that overwhelms a human coder's mental model often sits well within reach for a mind built to process breadth in parallel.
That gap points to something bigger than just this one project. Systems humans could only sketch on a whiteboard and never fully implement solo now become buildable, because the state-tracking load moves onto a mind actually built for that particular kind of heavy lifting. QuackSink stands as a solid example of exactly that shift in what becomes possible when the work gets split according to each kind of mind's actual strengths.
